In Spring, a **bean** is an object managed by the Spring IoC (Inversion of Control) container. Beans can be configured in **three ways**:

✅ **1. Using `@Component`, `@Service`, or `@Repository` (Annotation-Based)**

- Automatically detects beans via **component scanning**.
- Example:Spring automatically detects `MyBean` and registers it.

    ```java
    @Component
    public class MyBean {
        public void doSomething() { System.out.println("Bean Created!"); }}
    ```


✅ **2. Using `@Bean` in a Configuration Class (Java-Based Configuration)**

- Manually declares beans inside a **Spring `@Configuration` class**.

    ```java
    @Configuration
    public class AppConfig {
        @Bean
        public MyBean myBean() {
            return new MyBean();    }}
    ```

- ~~✅ **3. Using `beans.xml` (XML-Based Configuration - Deprecated in Modern Spring)**~~

    ```xml
    <beans>
        <bean id="myBean" class="com.example.MyBean"/>
    </beans>
    ```

