**Fictional example: customers and orders**

```java
@Entity
class Customer {
    @Id @GeneratedValue Long id;
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "customer", cascade = CascadeType.ALL,
               orphanRemoval = true)
    List<PurchaseOrder> orders = new ArrayList<>();
    void addOrder(PurchaseOrder order) {
        orders.add(order);
        order.customer = this;
    }
}
@Entity
@Table(name = "purchase_orders")
class PurchaseOrder {
    @Id @GeneratedValue Long id;
    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "customer_id", nullable = false)
    Customer customer;
}
```

The order owns the foreign key; `mappedBy` identifies the inverse side. Keep both sides consistent. Cascade and orphan removal are lifecycle choices, not fetch strategies. For a many-to-many example, use students, courses, and an Enrollment entity when the association has a grade or enrollment date. These entities are fictional.

## Integrated examples and clarifications

For bidirectional relationships, identify the owning side and keep both Java references consistent. In a typical parent/child mapping, the child `@ManyToOne` owns the foreign key and the parent `@OneToMany(mappedBy=...)` is inverse. Cascade and orphan removal are separate lifecycle decisions.

