1. Eager initialization: `private static final X INSTANCE = new X()`.
2. Double-checked locking with `volatile`:

```java
public final class Singleton {
    private static volatile Singleton instance;
    private Singleton() {}
    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                if (instance == null) instance = new Singleton();
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

3. Initialization-on-demand holder: lazy and thread-safe through class initialization.
4. Enum singleton: protects against ordinary reflective construction and preserves singleton identity during serialization.

## Integrated examples and clarifications

The older notes compare eager and lazy initialization and discuss reflection, serialization, and cloning. Prefer an enum or holder for ordinary use. A simple constructor guard is not a universal defense against reflective construction. A serializable class-based singleton can use readResolve to return its canonical instance.

## Implementation examples

```java
public final class JsonMapperHolder {
    private JsonMapperHolder() {}
    private static class Holder {
        static final ObjectMapper INSTANCE =
            new ObjectMapper().findAndRegisterModules();
    }
    public static ObjectMapper get() { return Holder.INSTANCE; }
}

public enum IdGenerator {
    INSTANCE;
    public String next() { return UUID.randomUUID().toString(); }
}
```

Class initialization makes the holder lazy and thread-safe. See 2.2 for double-checked locking.

