A many-to-many relationship involves an intermediary table to store associations between two entities.

1. **Use `@ManyToMany` Annotation**: This annotation defines the relationship between the two entities.
2. **Use `@JoinTable` to specify the join table**: This is required to store the association in a third table, which will link the two entities.

```java
@Entity
public class Product {
    @ManyToMany
    @JoinTable(
        name = "product_store",
        joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "product_id"),
        inverseJoinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "store_id")    )
    private Set<Store> stores = new HashSet<>();}

@Entity
public class Store {
    @ManyToMany(mappedBy = "stores")
    private Set<Product> products = new HashSet<>();}

✅ `@ManyToOne` and `@OneToMany` create a relationship between two entities (e.g., A Department has multiple Employees).

```java
@Entity
@Data
class Employee {
    @Id @GeneratedValue
    private Long id;
    private String name;

    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "department_id") // Foreign Key Column
    private Department department;}

@Entity
@Data
class Department {
    @Id @GeneratedValue
    private Long id;
    private String name;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "department", cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    private List<Employee> employees = new ArrayList<>();}

