In Spring, a **bean** is an object managed by the Spring IoC (Inversion of Control) container. Beans can be configured in **three ways**:

✅ **1. Using `@Component`, `@Service`, or `@Repository` (Annotation-Based)**

- Automatically detects beans via **component scanning**.
- Example:Spring automatically detects `MyBean` and registers it.

    ```java
    @Component
    public class MyBean {
        public void doSomething() { System.out.println("Bean Created!"); }}
    ```


✅ **2. Using `@Bean` in a Configuration Class (Java-Based Configuration)**

- Manually declares beans inside a **Spring `@Configuration` class**.

    ```java
    @Configuration
    public class AppConfig {
        @Bean
        public MyBean myBean() {
            return new MyBean();    }}
    ```

- ~~✅ **3. Using `beans.xml` (XML-Based Configuration - Deprecated in Modern Spring)**~~

    ```xml
    <beans>
        <bean id="myBean" class="com.example.MyBean"/>
    </beans>

- Singleton `A` **is created once**, and every instance of `B` gets the same `A`.

```java
@Component
@Scope("singleton")
public class A { }

@Component
@Scope("prototype")
public class B {
    private final A a;

    @Autowired
    public B(A a) { this.a = a; }
}

❌ **Direct injection of a `prototype` bean (`B`) into a `singleton` bean (`A`) causes problems.**

- Since `A` is a singleton, it will only get **one instance of `B` at startup**.

✅ **Solution: Use `ObjectProvider` (Recommended)**

```java
Component
@Scope("singleton")
public class A {
    private final ObjectProvider<B> bProvider;

    @Autowired
    public A(ObjectProvider<B> bProvider) {
        this.bProvider = bProvider;
    }

    public B getNewB() {
        return bProvider.getObject(); // Always gets a new instance of B
    }}
```

✅ **Alternative Solution: Use `@Lookup` Method Injection**

```java
@Component
@Scope("singleton")
public class A {
    @Lookup
    public B createB() { return null; } // Spring overrides this method at runtime
}

- **`@Autowired` (Dependency Injection)**
    - **Automatically injects a bean** where needed.
    - Uses **Spring IoC Container** to resolve dependencies.
- **How Does Spring Know Which Bean to Inject?**
    1. **By Type** → Finds a matching bean based on the class.
    2. **By Name** → If multiple beans of the same type exist, Spring uses the variable name.
    3. **Using `@Qualifier`** → Helps specify the exact bean to use.

🔹 **Example of `@Qualifier` for Multiple Beans:**

```java
@Component("dieselEngine")
public class DieselEngine implements Engine { }

@Component("electricEngine")
public class ElectricEngine implements Engine { }

@Component
public class Car {
    @Autowired
    @Qualifier("dieselEngine")
    private Engine engine; }// Explicitly inject DieselEngine

- **Circular Dependency occurs when two or more beans depend on each other**, creating an infinite loop.

### **How to Solve Circular Dependency?**

1. **Use `@Lazy` (Delays Bean Initialization)**
2. **Use Constructor Injection (`@Autowired` on Constructor)**

    ```java
    @Component
    public class A {@Autowired @Lazy private B b;}

    @Component
    public class A {
        private final B b;

        @Autowired
        public A(B b) { this.b = b; }}

- **Spring Boot provides `@Transactional` to manage transactions automatically.**

```java
@Service
public class BankService {
    @Autowired private AccountRepository accountRepo;

    @Transactional
    public void transferMoney(Long fromAccount, Long toAccount, Double amount) {
        Account sender = accountRepo.findById(fromAccount).get();
        sender.setBalance(sender.getBalance() - amount);
        accountRepo.save(sender);

        Account receiver = accountRepo.findById(toAccount).get();
        receiver.setBalance(receiver.getBalance() + amount);
        accountRepo.save(receiver);
    }}

- Spring Boot provides `@Valid` and `@Validated` annotations for validation.

```java
public class User {
    @NotBlank(message = "Name is required")
    private String name;

    @Email(message = "Invalid email format")
    private String email;}

@PostMapping("/users")
public ResponseEntity<String> createUser(@Valid @RequestBody User user, BindingResult result) {
    if (result.hasErrors()) {
        return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body(result.getAllErrors().toString());
    }
    return ResponseEntity.ok("User created");}
```

